int lcs(String a, String b) {
    int m = b.length();
    int[] dp = new int[m + 1];
    for (int i = 1; i <= a.length(); i++)
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++)
            dp[j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? dp[j - 1] + 1
                    : Math.max(dp[j], dp[j - 1]);
    return dp[m];
}

hard.why:
On a match, LCS needs the **diagonal** `dp[i-1][j-1]`, but going left to right, `dp[j - 1]` has already been overwritten with this row's `dp[i][j-1]`. For a = "a", b = "aa": j = 1 sets dp[1] = 1, then j = 2 matches and uses that new dp[1], giving 2, so the single 'a' is matched twice. The fix keeps a `diag` variable per row:

```java
int diag = 0;                       // old dp[j-1]
for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {
    int old = dp[j];
    dp[j] = match ? diag + 1 : Math.max(dp[j], dp[j - 1]);
    diag = old;
}
```

Looping j backwards is the tempting knapsack habit and even fixes this example, but then a mismatch reads `dp[j - 1]` from the previous row instead of the current row's left cell: `lcs("aa", "aba")` returns 1 instead of 2. `dp[0]` is always 0 already, and -1 changes nothing useful.

Takeaway: when a 1D recurrence needs up, left **and** diagonal, no loop direction gives you all three; save the diagonal explicitly (or keep two rows).

hard.code:
int change(int amount, int[] coins) {
    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
    dp[0] = 1;
    for (int s = 1; s <= amount; s++)
        for (int c : coins)
            if (c <= s) dp[s] += dp[s - c];
    return dp[amount];
}
